  "query_preview": "..\n\nSunny skies today with near to just slightly above normal\ntemperatures in the mid 70s to low 80s due to building high\npressure. Tranquil conditions will continue through tomorrow \nthen a change in the pattern will bring back chances for \nshowers. \n\nOver the next 7 to 10 days a series of troughs will move \nthrough the region leading to several rounds of precipitation \nchances. First, a longwave trough will move into the area from \nthe Pacific Northwest starting Thursday night. Within this \ntrough, a shortwave will extend southeast, move into northern \nBaja and swing into far southern Arizona Friday. The main trough\nwill move through northern Arizona Saturday. This will bring \nchances for rain showers focused to areas south of Tucson along \nthe International Border Friday then north of Tucson Saturday. \nFriday, there will be some instability, enough for a rumble of \nthunder or two. Saturday instability is much less. Precipitation\ntotals with this trough will be around 0.1 to 0.25 inches with \nlocally higher amounts. Higher rainfall will be focused to the \nInternational Border and the mountains. As the shortwave trough \nexits the region, breezy west to southwest winds 10 to 15 mph \ngusting 25 to 30 mph is likely as well as cooler temperatures 6 \nto 10 degrees below normal. \n\nFollowing this first trough, there will be a brief transition\nperiod with quieter weather expected on Sunday. Late Monday a \nsecondary and more substantial trough will move into the area. \nThis trough again will move in from the Pacific Northwest and \nthis time sweep across the whole state. This will bring chances\nfor precipitation across the area with an emphasis to areas \nnorth and northeast of Tucson. The current forecast has \nprecipitation totals around 0.1 to 0.5 inches with locally \nhigher amounts. This system looks to bring with it cooler air in\nthe mid levels lowering the snow level to bring chances for \nflurries at the top of Mount Graham and the White Mountains. As \nthe second trough exits the region, breezy south to southwest \nwinds is likely. There is a decent spread in the models for this\nsecondary trough so confidence is not high yet on precipitation\ntotals and exact timing but confidence will improve as we get \ncloser in time to this system.",
